1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,070
Chai Corner now has five
outlets and a small head office.

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:07,259
But every single decision
still waits for Ravi.

3
00:00:07,809 --> 00:00:09,976
Outlet three runs out of cups.

4
00:00:09,976 --> 00:00:10,876
Call Ravi.

5
00:00:11,169 --> 00:00:13,890
The college outlet wants a student offer.

6
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:14,790
Call Ravi.

7
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,848
By the time he answers,
the customers have gone.

8
00:00:18,498 --> 00:00:20,925
One desk cannot run five outlets.

9
00:00:20,925 --> 00:00:23,073
Ravi must decide who decides.

10
00:00:23,773 --> 00:00:25,831
So, where should decisions be made?

11
00:00:26,131 --> 00:00:30,126
Centralization means decision
making power is kept at the top.

12
00:00:30,476 --> 00:00:35,131
Decentralization means authority is
spread out, to lower levels and units.

13
00:00:35,481 --> 00:00:39,730
Last time, we saw delegation:
one task, handed to one person.

14
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:45,286
Decentralization is delegation across
the whole organization, done by plan.

15
00:00:45,936 --> 00:00:48,181
But this is never all or nothing.

16
00:00:48,181 --> 00:00:49,420
Picture a slider.

17
00:00:49,770 --> 00:00:52,627
All the way left, the
boss decides everything.

18
00:00:52,977 --> 00:00:56,672
All the way right, every unit
decides everything for itself.

19
00:00:57,022 --> 00:00:59,896
No real organization sits at either end.

20
00:01:00,246 --> 00:01:03,285
Henri Fayol called it a
question of proportion.

21
00:01:03,285 --> 00:01:05,367
Each firm finds its own point.

22
00:01:06,017 --> 00:01:07,988
So which way should the slider lean?

23
00:01:08,338 --> 00:01:13,015
Lean central when the firm is small,
when rules must be the same everywhere,

24
00:01:13,015 --> 00:01:14,201
or in a crisis.

25
00:01:14,551 --> 00:01:18,882
Lean the other way when the firm is
large, spread over many places, or

26
00:01:18,882 --> 00:01:20,477
when things change fast.

27
00:01:21,127 --> 00:01:23,312
Here is how Ravi sets his slider.

28
00:01:23,662 --> 00:01:27,895
The menu and the prices stay with
head office, so every cup tastes and

29
00:01:27,895 --> 00:01:29,008
costs the same.

30
00:01:29,358 --> 00:01:33,615
Daily staffing and local offers
move out to each outlet manager.

31
00:01:33,965 --> 00:01:37,015
Meena at the bus stand
now plans her own shifts.

32
00:01:37,365 --> 00:01:40,771
And the college outlet starts
its student offer the same day.

33
00:01:41,421 --> 00:01:42,975
Each side has a price.

34
00:01:43,325 --> 00:01:47,312
Centralization gives tight control,
and one standard everywhere.

35
00:01:47,312 --> 00:01:50,815
But the top gets overloaded,
and decisions slow down.

36
00:01:51,165 --> 00:01:55,304
Decentralization gives faster
decisions, and trains new managers.

37
00:01:55,304 --> 00:01:58,895
But outlets can drift apart,
and control gets harder.

38
00:01:59,545 --> 00:02:00,486
Quick check.

39
00:02:00,486 --> 00:02:04,788
The bus stand outlet adds a helper
for a festival rush, without asking

40
00:02:04,788 --> 00:02:05,729
head office.

41
00:02:05,729 --> 00:02:06,826
Which is this?

42
00:02:08,726 --> 00:02:09,818
Decentralization!

43
00:02:09,818 --> 00:02:13,891
A daily staffing call was made
at the outlet, close to the work.

44
00:02:14,491 --> 00:02:15,500
So, remember.

45
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:18,675
Centralization keeps decisions at the top.

46
00:02:18,675 --> 00:02:21,052
Decentralization spreads them down.

47
00:02:21,402 --> 00:02:23,833
It is a slider, a matter of degree.

48
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:26,124
No firm is fully one or the other.

49
00:02:26,474 --> 00:02:29,632
Small, uniform, or in
a crisis, lean central.

50
00:02:29,632 --> 00:02:33,320
Large, spread out, or fast
changing, lean decentralized.

51
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:35,937
Next time, span of control.

52
00:02:35,937 --> 00:02:38,993
How many people can one
manager really handle?
